\setlength{\emergencystretch}{2em}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\title{Eight squares through every edge}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
Write $n(k,4,\lambda)$ for the least order of a simple graph of degree
$k$, girth four and exactly $\lambda$ squares through every edge, with
value infinity if no such graph exists. We recover the following entry
of Table~4 of Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}:
\begin{theorem}\label{main}
\[n(6,4,8)\ge22.\]
\end{theorem}
The acquired tree bound gives order at least eighteen. The previously
reviewed twenty-vertex computation excludes that order. The additional
finite searches below handle the other orders. At twenty-one vertices,
a shortest odd cycle can have length seven, so a five-cycle search alone
would not suffice. The equality case of the cycle incidence bound
settles this extra possibility.

\begin{lemma}\label{equality}
Let $G$ be a triangle-free $k$-regular graph on $n$ vertices whose
shortest odd cycle has length $\ell$. If $k\ell=2n$, then $k$ is even
and $G$ is obtained from the cycle of length $\ell$ by replacing each
vertex by an independent set of size $k/2$ and every cycle edge by
all edges between the corresponding sets.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The acquired odd-cycle incidence argument says that a vertex has at
most two neighbours on a shortest odd cycle $C$. Equality in the
sum of these incidences forces every vertex to have exactly two.
Write $C=(v_0,\ldots,v_{\ell-1})$, with indices modulo $\ell$.
This cycle has no chord, since a chord would cut off a shorter odd
cycle. For a vertex outside $C$, its two neighbours divide $C$ into
one odd and one even arc. The odd arc together with that vertex is
an odd cycle, so the odd arc has length at least $\ell-2$. The other
arc has length at least two by triangle-freeness. Thus the latter
has length exactly two. The same description holds for cycle vertices.
Consequently the sets
\[
 A_i=\{x:N(x)\cap V(C)=\{v_{i-1},v_{i+1}\}\}
\]
partition the vertex set, and each contains $v_i$. Put $a_i=|A_i|$.
The degree of $v_i$ gives $a_{i-1}+a_{i+1}=k$. These equations imply
$a_{i+4}=a_i$. Since $\ell$ is odd, addition by four generates its
indices. Hence all $a_i$ equal $k/2$, proving that $k$ is even.

Each $A_i$ is independent because its vertices share a cycle neighbour.
We next show that an edge between different classes can join only
consecutive classes. Orient the indices so a putative edge $xy$ with
$x\in A_i$, $y\in A_j$ has $j-i=d$ where
$1\le d\le(\ell-1)/2$. If $d=2$, the common neighbour $v_{i+1}$
gives a triangle. If $d\ge4$ is even, use the arc from $v_{i+1}$ to
$v_{j-1}$ of length $d-2$, the two edges from its endpoints to $x,y$,
and $xy$. This is an odd cycle of length $d+1<\ell$. If $d\ge3$
is odd, instead use the complementary arc from $v_{i-1}$ to
$v_{j+1}$ of length $\ell-d-2$. It gives an odd cycle of length
$\ell-d+1<\ell$. These are simple cycles: the indicated arcs avoid
$v_i,v_j$, the only possible cycle vertices $x,y$. Both alternatives
contradict the choice of $C$.

Thus a vertex in $A_i$ can meet only $A_{i-1}\cup A_{i+1}$.
This union has exactly $k$ vertices. Its degree is $k$, so it meets
every one of them, proving the asserted description.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{finite}
For $n=18,19,21$, there is no simple triangle-free graph of order $n$
and degree six which contains a five-cycle and has eight squares
through every edge.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We use the previously proved cycle partition and exact completion
method~\cite{eighteen,twenty}. For a named five-cycle define $A_i$ as
above, define $B_i$ by the single neighbour $v_i$, and let $Z$ consist
of vertices with no cycle neighbour. Their sizes satisfy
\[
 a_{i-1}+a_{i+1}+b_i=6,\qquad
 \sum_i b_i+2z=2n-30,\qquad a_i\ge1,\quad b_i,z\ge0.
\]
The acquired inventory enumerates every weak composition of
$2n-30-2z$ into the five $b_i$, solves the five linear equations for
$a$, and identifies only rotations and reflections of the named
cycle. Independent enumeration of all $a\in\{1,\ldots,6\}^5$
checks the full labelled inventory before these identifications.

For each pattern the acquired search inserts precisely the prescribed
cycle edges and completes the other degrees. Its two reviewed
reductions permute active vertices with identical current
neighbourhoods, and bound the square count on every edge whose
endpoints have already reached their final degrees. They preserve
existence up to isomorphism. At every leaf all edges are checked
for the constant count eight. The proof of these reductions is
unchanged. The literal implementation differs from the reviewed
one only by permitting order twenty-one; its arrays have length
twenty-four and all unsigned masks still use fewer than thirty-two bits.

The complete computations are as follows. Each row has zero retained
graphs, and both ordinary and address/undefined-sanitizer builds agree
on every case and node count.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{r|r|r|r}
Order & Labelled size patterns & Dihedral representatives & Search nodes\\\hline
18 &191&27&2\,421\\
19 &306&42&45\,550\\
21 &477&65&79\,152\,873
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The attached records contain the input and complete output for each
case. A timeout has a distinct status and is rejected by the checker;
no unfinished case contributes to this table. The inventory,
completeness proof and exact zero outputs establish the lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{main}]
The acquired tree bound is
\[
 n\ge12+\left\lceil\frac{2(25-8)}6\right\rceil=18.
\]
For a bipartite graph with parts of size $m$, the acquired
Cauchy--Schwarz estimate gives
\[
 \lambda\ge \frac{150}{m-1}-5.
\]
A regular bipartite graph has equal part sizes, so odd orders are
impossible. At eighteen and twenty vertices the estimate gives
$\lambda\ge14$ and $\lambda\ge12$, respectively. None has value eight.

For a nonbipartite graph of order at most twenty-one, the shortest
odd cycle has length $\ell$ with $6\ell\le2n$. At orders eighteen,
nineteen and twenty this forces $\ell=5$. Lemma~\ref{finite}
excludes the first two; the reviewed twenty-vertex computation
\cite{twenty} excludes the third for square count eight.
At order twenty-one either $\ell=5$, again excluded by
Lemma~\ref{finite}, or $\ell=7$ and equality holds in the incidence
bound. Lemma~\ref{equality} then gives seven independent triples,
with complete adjacency only between consecutive triples.

For an edge between two consecutive triples, its opposite
neighbourhoods have sizes $3+2$ and $2+3$. Their cross edges number
\[
 3\cdot2+2\cdot2+2\cdot3=16.
\]
This is its square count, which is not eight. We have excluded every
order from eighteen through twenty-one, including both odd orders.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Attribution and certificates.}
The numerical lower bound is a prior result in Table~4 of~\cite{GJ},
physical page~20. The tree bound, odd-cycle incidence bound, bipartite
estimate, five-cycle inventory and completion method are acquired with
their actual earlier proofs. The contribution here is the explicit
odd-cycle equality argument and the complete applications to the three
remaining orders. No historical priority or open-conjecture claim is
made. An independent elementary check constructs the seven triples,
counts all squares and verifies their common value sixteen. The finite
search retains the known fifteen-vertex positive control, rejects
malformed inputs and distinguishes timeout from completion. This is
a computational proof with a written completeness argument; it is not
a Lean formalization and does not complete all results of~\cite{GJ}.

